WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-CA

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.200
These are the four most powerful writing&nbsp;
frameworks that I've ever come across. As an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:10.960
English professor and writing coach, I've spent&nbsp;
my career analyzing the world's best writers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:15.560
and what I'm going to give to you&nbsp;
here are four writing patterns that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:18.920
once you see them, you're going&nbsp;
to start spotting them everywhere.

00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:22.960
So today, I want to offer to you a&nbsp;
writing framework that'll help you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.960 --> 00:00:27.800
keep your readers focused. A framework&nbsp;
that'll give your writing momentum.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:33.840
A framework that'll help you break down&nbsp;
complex ideas into simpler units. And a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:39.800
framework that'll help you weave together&nbsp;
multiple strands into a single idea.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:45.480
Now, these frameworks are not just style tips&nbsp;
or my own list of personal preferences. They're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.480 --> 00:00:52.240
the cognitive patterns that readers use&nbsp;
to interpret your text. And by analyzing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:56.440
the writing patterns from the&nbsp;
perspective of reader psychology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.440 --> 00:01:02.080
it's going to help you write more clearly&nbsp;
and, more importantly, more valuably.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:06.960
So to start, let's take a look at the&nbsp;
same passage written two different ways,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:12.800
because this is going to let you in on the&nbsp;
principle that underlies all four patterns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:17.920
The original passage is taken from Mihaly&nbsp;
Csikszentmihalyi. Csikszentmihalyi — he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:23.120
the guy who wrote about flow. But before I get&nbsp;
to his original passage from the book *Flow*,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:27.840
what I'd like to do is look at the same passage&nbsp;
written differently, because it's the difference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:32.000
between them that's going to help you see&nbsp;
this principle that we're talking about.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:35.200
So my edit, the bad version, says:

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:41.200
&gt; Things that happen are not what produces&nbsp;
happiness. Good fortune and random chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:46.680
don't result in it either. Money can't&nbsp;
buy it and power can't command it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:52.680
Outside events do not determine it. Our&nbsp;
interpretation of those events does.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:59.280
Preparation, cultivation, and private defense&nbsp;
by each person are what this condition requires.

00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:03.120
Now, if I asked you what&nbsp;
was wrong with that passage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:08.960
it might be hard to say. I mean, there's no&nbsp;
grammar mistakes in it. It has all the pieces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:14.120
that we need to understand what he's talking&nbsp;
about, but something seems a little bit off.

00:02:14.120 --> 00:02:19.400
So let's contrast that with the original&nbsp;
from Csikszentmihalyi's book, which says:

00:02:19.400 --> 00:02:25.560
&gt; Happiness is not something that happens. It is&nbsp;
not the result of good fortune or random chance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:30.960
It is not something that money can buy or power&nbsp;
command. It does not depend on outside events,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:37.040
but rather on how we interpret them. Happiness,&nbsp;
in fact, is a condition that must be prepared for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:40.440
cultivated, and defended privately by each person.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:45.120
Now, speaking of flow, I think you'd agree&nbsp;
that that latter passage was a lot easier&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.120 --> 00:02:49.160
to understand. And you might have spotted&nbsp;
the difference, but in case you haven't,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:55.440
the key difference between these two passages is&nbsp;
that in the one that's difficult to understand,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.440 --> 00:03:02.840
we find all these different elements organized in&nbsp;
the topic position. At the start of the sentence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:08.800
we see *things that happen*, *good fortune*,&nbsp;
*money*, *outside events*, *preparation*.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.800 --> 00:03:14.120
And so if you were to look at just these&nbsp;
elements in the topic position without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:19.960
reading the rest of the passage, could&nbsp;
you predict what this passage is about?

00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:25.880
Well, let's contrast that with the topics that we&nbsp;
find in Csikszentmihalyi's version. We see in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:29.880
topic position of these clauses: *happiness*,&nbsp;
*happiness*, *happiness*, *happiness*,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:36.040
*happiness*. So if you were to zoom out and just&nbsp;
say, what is this passage about — the one in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.040 --> 00:03:42.000
all these sentences are joining together — what's&nbsp;
it about? It's really easy to say: happiness.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:46.200
And so the difference between the passage&nbsp;
that's difficult to read and easy to read&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:50.680
has nothing to do with grammar and&nbsp;
everything to do with organization.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.680 --> 00:03:57.280
The information is organized so that old,&nbsp;
familiar information is in the topic position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:02.760
and then all the new information comes at the&nbsp;
end of the sentence in what's called the comment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:09.000
And an easy way to think about this topic and&nbsp;
comment is to think that the topic of a clause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.000 --> 00:04:15.960
is kind of like a doorstep, and then the comment&nbsp;
is the room that the reader is going to walk into.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:20.520
And so with that image in mind, let me tell&nbsp;
you about the first writing framework. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:26.000
is the perfect framework to use whenever&nbsp;
you want to elaborate on a single topic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.000
to add more and more details to it. And in fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:33.120
it's the pattern that Csikszentmihalyi&nbsp;
used in the passage that we just looked at.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.920
So returning to that passage,&nbsp;
we see the first sentence says,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:42.040
"Happiness is not something that happens." And&nbsp;
so in the topic position of this first sentence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:48.680
we have the word *happiness*. And then we see all&nbsp;
these pronouns and the word *happiness* repeated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:55.240
recurring through every successive clause. All&nbsp;
the new, complex information is developed in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.240 --> 00:05:01.560
comment position, and all the topics are coherent.&nbsp;
They're all linking back to the same element.

00:05:01.560 --> 00:05:05.760
And so if you hold on to that image&nbsp;
of the topic being like a doorstep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:10.280
then this first pattern is kind of like&nbsp;
you're having the reader stand there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:14.760
and look in the different cardinal&nbsp;
directions. There are multiple ideas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:20.320
being developed and attached to this&nbsp;
single element that keeps in focus.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:26.840
So, as you see, this pattern is what allows us&nbsp;
to stay anchored on one topic while developing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:32.480
multiple ideas about it. And it's perfect&nbsp;
for whenever you are describing something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.480 --> 00:05:40.440
or characterizing something, or just developing&nbsp;
a multifaceted idea. You don't want the reader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:46.920
to get lost in all the details, and so this&nbsp;
pattern allows you to keep the reader anchored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:52.800
on the one element that you want them to focus&nbsp;
on. And from that vantage point, they'll be able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:58.960
to integrate all these ideas that you develop,&nbsp;
as we see here in the passage about happiness.

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:03.240
Now, this pattern is really handy,&nbsp;
but you should be warned that it can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.240 --> 00:06:09.080
create a kind of stagnant feeling in the&nbsp;
passage. So you really want to reserve it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:14.040
for moments whenever you really need&nbsp;
to elaborate on something and unpack&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:23.240
multiple ideas about a single entity. But beware&nbsp;
that it is going to make your reader feel stuck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:28.000
because they're standing on the doorstep and&nbsp;
they're looking in these different directions.

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:31.320
But of course, you don't always want the&nbsp;
reader to be standing still. Sometimes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:35.960
you want to give them some movement, and that's&nbsp;
what the next framework is for. And by the way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.960 --> 00:06:40.360
if you want to take a closer look at these&nbsp;
frameworks, then you can get my free visual guide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:45.720
to expert writing down below the like button.&nbsp;
It contains not only these four frameworks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:50.840
but actually four pages of frameworks and&nbsp;
patterns that I've used to help writers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.840 --> 00:06:55.920
make their writing more clear and more valuable.&nbsp;
Totally free. Link is down below the like button.

00:06:55.920 --> 00:07:01.200
So what is this second framework, and how can you&nbsp;
use it to give your writing a feeling of momentum?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.200 --> 00:07:06.760
Well, in the last section, we talked about&nbsp;
the framework being as though you have the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.760 --> 00:07:11.880
reader stood on a doorstep and then turning&nbsp;
them into different cardinal directions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:18.640
But with this framework, you want to imagine that&nbsp;
from the doorstep, the reader steps into a room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:23.600
and then from that room, they're now standing&nbsp;
in a new room. They have a new vantage point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:29.080
and the next clause brings them into the next&nbsp;
room. Right? It's one step after the other,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.080 --> 00:07:33.880
and they're successive steps, right? These&nbsp;
aren't teleportations. We're not jerking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.880 --> 00:07:38.760
the reader around the house from one room&nbsp;
to another. Everything is linking one into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:42.360
the other. And in fact, that's what the&nbsp;
pattern is called: the linking pattern.

00:07:42.360 --> 00:07:46.240
So let me give you an example of how&nbsp;
this works. This comes from James Gleick,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:50.160
in his excellent book *Chaos: Making&nbsp;
a New Science*. So the passage says:

00:07:50.160 --> 00:07:53.960
&gt; Outside his window, Lorenz&nbsp;
could watch real weather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:58.920
the early morning fog creeping along the&nbsp;
Massachusetts Institute of Technology campus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.920 --> 00:08:02.760
or the low clouds slipping over&nbsp;
the roofs from the Atlantic.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:09.120
Fog and clouds never arose in the model running&nbsp;
on his computer. The machine, a Royal McBee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:15.480
was a thicket of wiring and vacuum tubes that&nbsp;
occupied an ungainly portion of Lorenz's office.

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:18.080
And so what we see with this framework is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:26.280
each topic is linking back not to the original&nbsp;
topic, but to the end of the previous clause.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.280 --> 00:08:30.840
That is, it's picking up the element&nbsp;
that's in the comment and using that as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.840 --> 00:08:35.920
the next starting point, and that's what&nbsp;
gives it this sense of forward momentum.

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:40.920
So we see the first topic is "outside&nbsp;
his window" — Lorenz would be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.920 --> 00:08:47.440
the first topic — and at the end we have two&nbsp;
elements in the comment position. We have *fog*&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:52.360
and we have *clouds*. Those are the&nbsp;
elements that are in the comment position,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.360 --> 00:08:59.280
and then those elements get repeated. They're&nbsp;
linked back in the next topic, right? We see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:05.560
"fog and clouds." What about them? "They never&nbsp;
arose in the model running on his computer."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:11.840
And then *computer* becomes the topic of the next&nbsp;
clause: "The machine, a Royal McBee, was a thicket&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.840 --> 00:09:18.080
of wiring." And notice that at the end of this&nbsp;
clause, we are now ending back where we began.

00:09:18.080 --> 00:09:24.120
Now, that's not absolutely necessary, but it's&nbsp;
a nice touch, and I'm going to give you another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.120 --> 00:09:30.440
example in a moment to show what can happen when&nbsp;
you don't kind of close this loop. But just for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:36.280
the moment, notice what's happening and how this&nbsp;
gives the passage this kind of forward motion.

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:43.920
This kind of a pattern is excellent for whenever&nbsp;
you want the reader to be moving through space. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:50.400
that could be in a passage where you're describing&nbsp;
travel, but it's also excellent — you know, I work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:56.200
in academia, and so this is a really good pattern&nbsp;
for the method section of a scientific report.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.200 --> 00:10:02.360
But it's really useful for anything that involves&nbsp;
process, you know, one step after another. If you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.360 --> 00:10:08.040
need to show any causal chain, this is the&nbsp;
kind of framework that you'd want to use.

00:10:08.040 --> 00:10:11.000
But a moment ago, I mentioned that,&nbsp;
you know, this is a nice touch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:18.720
the way Lorenz — the comment of this passage&nbsp;
— links back up to the topic where we began.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:24.680
That's a nice touch, because watch what happens&nbsp;
if we don't have that kind of a closed loop.

00:10:24.680 --> 00:10:29.360
Here's the same pattern. It's&nbsp;
exactly the same linking pattern,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.360 --> 00:10:34.120
but what you're going to notice is that it kind of&nbsp;
falls apart. And I wonder if you can see why. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.120 --> 00:10:38.080
this passage is not from professional prose. It's&nbsp;
just something I whipped up to prove a point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:41.000
but see if you can spot where it&nbsp;
goes off the rails. So it says:

00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:47.520
&gt; Soon, Portland will be the coffee roasting hub&nbsp;
of the nation. The aroma of roasting beans fills&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:53.120
the streets, and gleaming espresso machines line&nbsp;
the counters. The machines remind me of my dad's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:59.280
old typewriter, covered with keys I would press&nbsp;
randomly. His typewriter always jammed on me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.280 --> 00:11:07.800
That's why I avoided it. I prefer to write&nbsp;
with blue pen on yellow legal pads. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.800 --> 00:11:12.880
preference has been the topic of heated debates&nbsp;
between me and my creative writing professor.

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:18.320
All right. Now, this is exactly the same pattern.&nbsp;
We have the topic picking up the comment of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:25.480
previous clause. But the problem is that when&nbsp;
you zoom out and you look at these topics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:31.160
just as we did at the start here — when you&nbsp;
just analyze the topics, can you tell me what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.160 --> 00:11:38.440
this passage is about? Right. Let's take a closer&nbsp;
look. All right. This passage is about *Portland*,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.000 --> 00:11:44.520
*coffee beans*, *espresso machines*, *typewriter*,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:52.120
*that*, *I*, *preference*, *creative&nbsp;
writing professor*. Like, it's hard to see.

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:58.320
One way to think about it is to say it's&nbsp;
hard to picture these elements on the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.320 --> 00:12:04.200
stage. If these are all characters in a&nbsp;
play, then you might ask yourself, like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:08.680
what are they doing together, right?&nbsp;
So what is this passage about?

00:12:08.680 --> 00:12:15.000
And that's what happens. If you start linking all&nbsp;
these clauses together, you get a nice experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:21.240
of flow or momentum, but as a writer, you kind&nbsp;
of need to keep them on track. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.240 --> 00:12:27.680
you need to keep everything coherent. So it&nbsp;
works in this passage about Lorenz because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.680 --> 00:12:33.440
the author is kind of taking us on a guided&nbsp;
tour of his office. We start looking at Lorenz,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:38.240
and then we kind of zoom out a little bit&nbsp;
and look at the context of the environment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.240 --> 00:12:43.040
We're kind of floating around in space,&nbsp;
and then we're brought back to Lorenz.

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:45.160
Now, again, I think there are&nbsp;
different ways that you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:50.360
use this. I don't think you necessarily&nbsp;
always need to loop back where you began,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:50.360 --> 00:12:55.200
but you do need to be careful about the&nbsp;
way that you use this pattern, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.200 --> 00:13:00.240
it's kind of like railroad tracks, and you&nbsp;
don't want it to take you too far off course.

00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:04.520
Now, for this third writing framework, you&nbsp;
might notice that it actually breaks a rule.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.520 --> 00:13:11.160
So far we've been talking about repetition, and&nbsp;
sometimes that repetition happens just through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.760 --> 00:13:19.840
repeating the word or using pronouns. And in other&nbsp;
cases we have synonyms being used. So for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:24.600
"the machine," right, and "the Royal McBee"&nbsp;
replacing "the machine," right — "his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:29.680
computer." So we can use synonyms, or&nbsp;
pronouns, or just straight repetition.

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:32.880
But for this third writing framework,&nbsp;
you're actually going to notice that it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.880 --> 00:13:38.840
breaks that rule entirely. We don't repeat&nbsp;
the same element in the topic positions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.840 --> 00:13:42.640
but it still works. So let me read it for&nbsp;
you and see if you can see how this one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.640 --> 00:13:46.440
works. This model passage, by the way,&nbsp;
comes from Steven Pinker, and it says:

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:51.520
&gt; When neuroscientists look directly at&nbsp;
the brain, using a variety of techniques,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.520 --> 00:13:55.440
they can actually see language&nbsp;
in action in the left hemisphere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.040 --> 00:14:01.680
The anatomy of the normal brain, its bulges&nbsp;
and creases, is slightly asymmetrical.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.280 --> 00:14:06.960
In some of the regions associated with language,&nbsp;
the differences are large enough to be seen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.960 --> 00:14:11.840
with the naked eye. Aphasics' brains almost&nbsp;
always show lesions in the left hemisphere.

00:14:11.840 --> 00:14:17.080
Right? So if we examine the topics&nbsp;
of all of these successive clauses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.080 --> 00:14:23.280
we find that there is no repetition. These are&nbsp;
all different elements, but somehow they do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.280 --> 00:14:27.800
seem to work together. I don't know about you,&nbsp;
but I found that passage pretty easy to read.

00:14:27.800 --> 00:14:32.040
And so what's the difference between this&nbsp;
passage and the one that we just looked at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:32.040 --> 00:14:35.920
about the Portland coffee beans? Right?&nbsp;
This one about the Portland coffee beans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.920 --> 00:14:42.400
seems pretty incoherent, yet this one&nbsp;
from Steven Pinker works. Why is that?

00:14:42.400 --> 00:14:46.960
Well, it's a little bit like walking into a&nbsp;
cathedral, right? If you walk into a cathedral,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.960 --> 00:14:52.360
you're not going to be very surprised to find&nbsp;
inside the nave, the crypt, and the pulpit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:52.360 --> 00:14:58.520
In other words, once the whole is on stage,&nbsp;
you're not going to be surprised to see its parts.

00:14:58.520 --> 00:15:03.720
And that's what's happening here. We see the whole&nbsp;
element being introduced in the comment position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.720 --> 00:15:10.400
— that being the left hemisphere, which is the&nbsp;
language center of the brain — and then different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.400 --> 00:15:17.800
aspects of that one element are introduced in all&nbsp;
the successive comments. We have "the anatomy of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:17.800 --> 00:15:23.640
the normal brain" being developed in one comment.&nbsp;
In the next clause we have "in some of the regions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.640 --> 00:15:29.600
associated with language," right, over here.&nbsp;
And then third we have "aphasics' brains."

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:34.720
So although these are different topics,&nbsp;
they're different elements — right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:42.040
the topics are indexing different things&nbsp;
— those three things are subsumed below&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.040 --> 00:15:47.640
the one topic, the kind of general category&nbsp;
that's introduced in the comment position.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:53.480
And this is an excellent pattern for whenever&nbsp;
you're providing a taxonomy, or just a kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.480 --> 00:16:00.640
of — you're categorizing something, or you're just&nbsp;
breaking down one element into its various parts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.240 --> 00:16:05.800
Although the parts are going to be&nbsp;
scattered and they're not going to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:13.760
renaming the same topic, whenever you provide&nbsp;
this kind of high-level introduction of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.760 --> 00:16:19.560
category on top of it, almost like an umbrella,&nbsp;
then it lends coherence to the whole passage.

00:16:19.560 --> 00:16:25.040
But for contrast, I think it's also helpful to&nbsp;
look at how this pattern can go wrong. So what I'd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:31.120
like to do now is just read you another passage&nbsp;
with something missing, and you can try to see if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.120 --> 00:16:36.120
you can tell what goes wrong with it. And so this&nbsp;
passage, which follows the same pattern, says:

00:16:36.120 --> 00:16:41.920
&gt; After gathering the smallest and most colorful&nbsp;
leaves from the maples and oaks in our backyard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:45.240
we place the leaves between&nbsp;
sheets of blotter paper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.240 --> 00:16:49.720
which we then cover with a large&nbsp;
heavy book. In just a day or two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.720 --> 00:16:55.760
the leaves are ready to be mounted on cards.&nbsp;
We use plain index cards folded in half.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.280 --> 00:17:02.640
Using clear adhesive paper, we put one leaf on&nbsp;
each card, leaving room for the guest's name.

00:17:02.640 --> 00:17:06.280
Now, the first question would be: how&nbsp;
was that? Was that easy to read? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:06.280 --> 00:17:09.400
I think we'd all agree no. There's&nbsp;
something wrong with that passage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.320 --> 00:17:14.320
(Mosquito attack.)

00:17:14.320 --> 00:17:18.160
And so what is it that's gone&nbsp;
wrong with this passage? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:18.160 --> 00:17:23.280
the thing that it's missing — and the reason&nbsp;
why it seems so kind of incomprehensible or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:23.280 --> 00:17:30.320
disjointed — is that it's missing this index&nbsp;
clause, right? If you're going to have a passage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:30.320 --> 00:17:37.760
which names these varying topics, you need to be&nbsp;
specific in naming the category, or the kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:37.760 --> 00:17:43.880
super-theme, that joins them all together.&nbsp;
If that theme is only implied — right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:43.880 --> 00:17:48.120
if you don't name it specifically where&nbsp;
readers are expecting to find it — then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:48.120 --> 00:17:53.160
they're going to find all the successive&nbsp;
clauses in this passage totally incoherent.

00:17:53.160 --> 00:17:58.160
And so if we were to introduce in the&nbsp;
index position a clause that kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.160 --> 00:18:02.320
hangs over them all as an umbrella,&nbsp;
suddenly they snap into place. Watch:

00:18:02.320 --> 00:18:07.880
&gt; Each Thanksgiving we make place cards decorated&nbsp;
with pressed autumn leaves. After gathering the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:07.880 --> 00:18:12.200
smallest and most colorful leaves from the&nbsp;
maples and oaks in our backyard, we place the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:12.200 --> 00:18:17.240
leaves between sheets of blotter paper, which&nbsp;
we then cover with a large heavy book. In just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:17.240 --> 00:18:23.480
a day or two, the leaves are ready to be mounted&nbsp;
on cards. We use plain index cards folded in half.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:23.480 --> 00:18:29.240
Using clear adhesive paper, we put one leaf on&nbsp;
each card, leaving room for the guest's name.

00:18:29.240 --> 00:18:33.280
Ah, like, see, they all snap&nbsp;
into place. So that tells you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:33.280 --> 00:18:38.200
the importance of having a kind of&nbsp;
unifying category on top of it all.

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:42.960
Now, this last writing framework is excellent&nbsp;
for whenever you need to weave together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.960 --> 00:18:48.640
multiple strands into a single cohesive thread.&nbsp;
And so, keeping with the metaphor that we've&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.640 --> 00:18:55.360
been developing here, if the topic is like the&nbsp;
doorstep where a reader arrives before we present&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:55.360 --> 00:19:02.080
them with something new, then the comment of this&nbsp;
last pattern is a little bit like a tour guide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:02.080 --> 00:19:07.960
approaching the reader and saying, "We're going to&nbsp;
go to this room, that room, and this other room."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.600 --> 00:19:14.360
In other words, it's giving them a preview of&nbsp;
the major turns that they're about to take.

00:19:14.360 --> 00:19:18.400
And so let's watch this in action, and&nbsp;
then I'll show you the framework and how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.400 --> 00:19:23.800
to use it. This example passage comes from&nbsp;
Jonathan Haidt. He's an excellent writer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:23.800 --> 00:19:27.440
and this passage occurs in his book&nbsp;
*The Righteous Mind*. And so it says:

00:19:27.440 --> 00:19:33.400
&gt; In their theory of morality, Shweder et al.&nbsp;
found three major clusters of moral themes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:33.400 --> 00:19:37.080
which they called the ethics of&nbsp;
autonomy, community, and divinity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.600 --> 00:19:42.000
The ethic of autonomy is based on the&nbsp;
idea that people are first and foremost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:42.000 --> 00:19:47.800
autonomous individuals with wants, needs, and&nbsp;
preferences. The ethic of community is based&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.800 --> 00:19:53.360
on the idea that people are first and foremost&nbsp;
members of larger entities such as families,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.360 --> 00:20:00.040
teams, armies, companies, tribes, and nations.&nbsp;
The ethic of divinity is based on the idea that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:00.040 --> 00:20:05.120
people are first and foremost temporary vessels&nbsp;
within which a divine soul has been implanted.

00:20:05.120 --> 00:20:10.360
And so what you might have spotted already is&nbsp;
that the comment of this first index clause&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.360 --> 00:20:16.080
sets up A, B, and C. And then&nbsp;
those elements that occur at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:16.080 --> 00:20:21.600
end of the comment each get picked up in&nbsp;
successive clauses. A gets developed here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.600 --> 00:20:26.040
B gets developed here, and C gets developed&nbsp;
here. So that's why it's called a theme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.040 --> 00:20:31.080
preview. It's giving the reader a heads-up of&nbsp;
the three major turns that we're about to take.

00:20:31.080 --> 00:20:38.160
But a failure to watch out for with this passage&nbsp;
is the use of synonyms. The reason this all snaps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.160 --> 00:20:46.800
together is because these words echo the key&nbsp;
terms that were put out in the comment position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:46.800 --> 00:20:52.240
of the first clause. If we were to use a&nbsp;
synonym here — *community*, such as, like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:52.240 --> 00:20:58.920
*groups* — well, it wouldn't have the same&nbsp;
cohesive structure as using the same term,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:58.920 --> 00:21:04.560
or a very close, not synonym, but&nbsp;
analog, like *community* and *communal*,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:04.560 --> 00:21:10.400
or *autonomy* and *autonomous*. Those are fine,&nbsp;
because they link back to the same key terms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:10.400 --> 00:21:17.000
But if you try to use synonyms here, you're really&nbsp;
kind of taxing the reader's mental framework,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:17.000 --> 00:21:22.240
because they have to keep track of these three&nbsp;
elements. And if you can help them keep track&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:22.240 --> 00:21:28.520
of that by repeating the same terms, then they&nbsp;
can snap the information into this structure.

00:21:28.520 --> 00:21:34.120
Now, we've been looking at these passages in the&nbsp;
context of paragraphs, but keep in mind that these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.120 --> 00:21:40.120
can occur at different levels of resolution, and&nbsp;
you can even mix and match them. So for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:40.840 --> 00:21:47.680
if you can expand this pattern out to an entire&nbsp;
section, right, you can imagine the introduction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:47.680 --> 00:21:54.640
of a section announcing three major themes, and&nbsp;
then several paragraphs being given to element A,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.640 --> 00:21:59.720
and several paragraphs being given to element&nbsp;
B, and so on and so forth, linking back with,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:59.720 --> 00:22:04.240
you know, major signposts like *first*,&nbsp;
*second*, *third* to help the reader keep track.

00:22:04.240 --> 00:22:09.080
Then within this macro structure,&nbsp;
you can introduce other patterns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:09.080 --> 00:22:14.640
such as the linking pattern, right? You&nbsp;
can go old to new, right? New information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:14.640 --> 00:22:19.080
gets picked up in the old information,&nbsp;
in that kind of stepped linking pattern,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:19.080 --> 00:22:23.920
in whatever way you can. So try to expand&nbsp;
this out into different levels of resolution.

00:22:23.920 --> 00:22:27.520
And when you're done, you need to watch&nbsp;
this video next, because it's going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.520 --> 00:22:33.200
give you five more patterns that I see not&nbsp;
only at the level of sentence organization,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.200 --> 00:22:36.800
but across the entire essay.&nbsp;
So be sure to go check it out.

